npm install failure investigation log — web-analyzer
Generated: 2026-08-30

SUMMARY
-------
Root cause: npm 10.9.2 hit "Exit handler never called!" (known npm CLI bug)
on every run. This crashes the install before it writes cached/downloaded
packages into node_modules, even when nearly everything succeeded. Two
things kept triggering it on this slow network connection:

  1. npm's own security-audit check (POST to registry.npmjs.org/-/npm/v1/
     security/audits/quick) timing out (ETIMEDOUT) on every run.
  2. @rolldown/binding-darwin-arm64 (an OPTIONAL native binary for the
     Rolldown bundler) consistently timing out after 7-8+ retries.

REQUIRED packages that failed at least one download attempt but eventually
cached successfully (safe — will install fine once the crash is avoided):
  @napi-rs/canvas, @oxc-project/types, bmp-js, detect-libc, fdir,
  idb-keyval, is-url, lightningcss, nanoid, opencollective-postinstall,
  picomatch, postcss, regenerator-runtime, rolldown, tesseract.js,
  tesseract.js-core, tinyglobby, typescript, vite, wasm-feature-detect,
  zlibjs

REQUIRED package still unresolved as of last check (not yet confirmed
cached — needs to be watched on the next run):
  @rolldown/pluginutils

OPTIONAL package skipped intentionally (native binary, not needed for the
app to run — Vite/Rolldown falls back without it):
  @rolldown/binding-darwin-arm64
  (plus all other-OS/arch @rolldown/binding-* and lightningcss-*/
  @napi-rs/canvas-* variants, which were never attempted since they don't
  match this machine's OS/arch)

FIX APPLIED (INTERMEDIATE, superseded below)
---------------------------------------------
Re-running with: npm install --no-audit --omit=optional
  --no-audit      -> stops npm from making the audit request that was
                      timing out and contributing to the crash
  --omit=optional -> skips @rolldown/binding-darwin-arm64 (and other
                      optional native binaries) entirely instead of
                      retrying it 10x, removing the other crash trigger

This got further but @rolldown/pluginutils and @oxc-project/types still
timed out consistently. Direct `curl` to the exact same URLs succeeded
instantly (~1s), ruling out real network/registry problems.

TRUE ROOT CAUSE (confirmed via node -e "fetch(...)" with full error.cause)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
This machine has NO working IPv6 route to registry.npmjs.org
(curl -6 fails instantly with "no route"/EHOSTUNREACH). That alone is
harmless -- curl's happy-eyeballs falls back to IPv4 immediately and
succeeds every time.

Node.js's own connection logic (`autoSelectFamily`, Node's Happy Eyeballs
implementation used internally by npm's fetch/undici) races IPv6 and IPv4
connection attempts across ALL resolved addresses (12 IPv4 + 12 IPv6
Cloudflare anycast IPs for this domain). On this machine, that race
somehow stalls/corrupts every single IPv4 attempt too (all 12 came back
ETIMEDOUT from Node, while curl succeeded on every one individually).
This produced the exact symptom pattern: whichever 1-2 packages npm
happened to be fetching, with --maxsockets=2, would get stuck retrying
forever, while everything fetched earlier (before this bug engaged) was
already cached.

FINAL FIX (confirmed working)
------------------------------
Set this env var before running npm/node commands in this project:

  NODE_OPTIONS="--dns-result-order=ipv4first --no-network-family-autoselection"

  --dns-result-order=ipv4first        -> prefer IPv4 addresses first
  --no-network-family-autoselection   -> disable the Happy Eyeballs
                                          race entirely; try one address
                                          at a time like curl does

With this set, a clean `rm -rf node_modules && npm install --no-audit
--omit=optional` completed in 6 seconds (vs. never completing before).

Baked into package.json's dev/build/preview/build-sprites scripts so
`npm run dev` etc. always work. NOT yet persisted for plain `npm install`
itself -- to fix that too, add this line to ~/.zshrc:

  export NODE_OPTIONS="--dns-result-order=ipv4first --no-network-family-autoselection"
